Q: How to separately implode each row of a 2d array A: <p>If it is a two dimensional array and would like to output all elements, you could use a foreach loop and output the implode of each like so:</p>

<pre><code>$mainArray = [
    [4],
    [3, 4],
    [2, 30, 43, 65, 53, 634]];

foreach($mainArray as $key =&gt; $secArray){
    echo implode('-', $secArray) . '&lt;br/&gt;';
}
</code></pre>

<p><a href="http://php.net/manual/en/function.implode.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer">PHP Implode</a></p>

<p>Notice the return type of implode is a string.</p>

Q: Java self referencing generic type A: <p>There are two issues with Self type that I can think of : contra variance of parameters, and constructors</p>
<h1>Contra-variance of parameters</h1>
<p>If a <em>Vehicle</em> can embark <em>People</em> , then a <em>Car</em> which is a <strong>subtype</strong> of <em>Vehicle</em> can also embark <em>People</em>. It may not just embark <em>Japanese</em> people, or just <em>Children</em>. It may however embark <em>Mobile</em> objects which is a <strong>supertype</strong> of <em>People</em>.</p>
<p>That is contra variance of parameters : in a <strong>sub</strong> type, the parameters must be a <strong>super</strong> type (can be the same)</p>
<p>Therefore, a Self type would be invalid as a parameter, since it would be a <strong>sub</strong> type in the child class. This means, Self can't be used for parameters</p>
<p>This is not a problem for return types, which are <em>covariant</em> instead : in a <strong>sub</strong> type, the return type must be a <strong>sub</strong> type (can be the same).<br />
If a <em>Vehicle</em> gives a <em>Receipt</em> upon embarking, then a <em>Car</em> can give a <em>PaperReceipt</em> , but not a supertype <em>Document</em> . Self is covariant so can fit the return type.</p>
<p>Therefore, Self should only be present as returned type, but there is another issue to build one.</p>
<h1>Constructing Self</h1>
<p>Let's say a <em>Vehicle</em> can be <code>clone()</code> ton return <em>Self</em> . How do you construct that Self ? Well you can't because any method that return a <code>new X()</code> will be invalid : let's say your <em>Car</em>'s <code>clone()</code> return a <code>new Car</code>, then your <em>ElectricCar</em> will also return a <em>Car</em> which is not <em>Self</em>, as it's supposed to return a <em>ElectricCar</em>.</p>
<p>You thus need to override each method returning Self, which defeats the point of OOP.</p>
<p>The only <em>Self</em> that you reliably have access to, is <code>this</code>. Which means that <em>Self</em> returning methods should be considered/written as <code>void</code> method that the compiler actually make return this at the end.</p>
<h1>Valid usage</h1>
<p>This is still pretty useful, for example</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>class NumberStore {
  Self writeOnly(){preventWrite=false ; preventRead=true;}
  Self withCacheExpires(int seconds){cacheExpires=seconds;}
}

class IntStore extends NumberStore{
  IntStore store(int value){this.value=value;}
}

class Test {
  void foo(){
    var myIntStore = new IntStore();
    // reduce the need to chain things manually
    myIntStore.writeOnly().withCacheExpire(12).store(12);
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>But is this small usage worth changing the language ? Well, I do think it is, but I'm not sure there is no other caveat.</p>
